main lifts; training to failure is not required."},{"claimId":"failure_proximity","decision":"Smaller exercises go a little closer to failure (about 1–2 repetitions in reserve) because growth tends to improve closer to failure."},{"claimId":"rest_hypertrophy","decision":"Rest about 3 minutes after main-lift sets and 75 seconds after smaller exercises."},{"claimId":"freq_strength","decision":"Each major muscle group is trained on at least two days, spreading the weekly sets so each session stays manageable."},{"claimId":"progress_both","decision":"Progress by adding repetitions first, then weight (double progression)."},{"claimId":"progress_increase","decision":"Load increases of about 2–10% once you can do 1–2 repetitions more than planned."},{"claimId":"deload_concept","decision":"A lighter week is planned as an option, used when tiredness or performance call for it."},{"claimId":"who_activity","decision":"150 minutes of cardio a week, inside the WHO range of 150–300."},{"claimId":"concurrent","decision":"Cardio goes on other days or at least a few hours away from lifting where possible, so it does not blunt your strength sessions."}],"inputs":{"goal":"strength","experience":"intermediate","daysPerWeek":3,"sessionMinutes":75,"equipment":["bodyweight","dumbbell","barbell","ez_bar","rack","bench","kettlebell","band","pullup_bar","dip_bars","box","landmine"],"age":30,"pain":[],"priorityMuscles":[],"currentActivity":"regular","screeningStatus":"ok"}}'::jsonb, 'library')
