es, Abs & obliques are below the weekly target because the sessions are full. More minutes per session or another training day would close the gap."}],"claimIds":["acsm_days","vol_fractional","vol_minimum","vol_session_cap","vol_targets_design","exercise_order","estimate_time","warmup","load_range","failure_optional","failure_proximity","rir_scale","rest_hypertrophy","freq_hypertrophy","progress_both","progress_increase","deload_concept","deload_rule","who_activity","concurrent","injury_prevention"],"rationale":[{"claimId":"acsm_days","decision":"3 strength days a week, matching the time you have."},{"claimId":"vol_fractional","decision":"Weekly sets are counted as 1 for the main muscle and ½ for helping muscles, and each muscle group gets a target range."},{"claimId":"vol_minimum","decision":"Targets start at about 4 weekly sets per muscle group, the time-efficient minimum, and go higher when time allows."},{"claimId":"exercise_order","decision":"Each session starts with the big multi-joint lifts and ends with smaller single-joint and core exercises."},{"claimId":"warmup","decision":"Every session starts with a short general warm-up and lighter practice sets."},{"claimId":"load_range","decision":"Main lifts use 8–12 repetitions and smaller exercises use higher repetitions; lighter and heavier loads both build muscle."},{"claimId":"failure_optional","decision":"Sets stop about 2 repetitions short of failure on the main lifts; training to failure is not required."},{"claimId":"failure_proximity","decision":"Smaller exercises go a little closer to failure (about 1–2 repetitions in reserve) because growth tends to improve closer to failure."},{"claimId":"rest_hypertrophy","decision":"Rest about 2.5 minutes after main-lift sets and 75 seconds after smaller exercises."},{"claimId":"freq_hypertrophy","decision":"Each major muscle group is trained on at least two days, spreading the weekly sets so each session stays manageable."},{"claimId":"progress_both","decision":"Prog